<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zapotecs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zapotec people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zapotecs are an indigenous people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Their ancestors built Monte Albán, which from about 500 BCE to 800 CE was the capital of one of the first states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, and later centres such as Mitla. In the Postclassic period Zapotec kingdoms such as Zaachila and Tehuantepec fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>. Zapotec languages are still spoken by several hundred thousand people, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> was Zapotec.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zapotecs.jpg" alt="Zapotecs" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BenjaminGandiaga · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ni%C3%B1os_Zapotecos_del_Istmo_de_Tehuantepec_escuchando_en_su_Lengua_Zapoteca_la_Convencion_Anual.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
